1
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:05,581
Case file number one:

2
00:00:05,581 --> 00:00:07,095
the vanishing paycheck.

3
00:00:07,355 --> 00:00:08,046
Every month,

4
00:00:08,046 --> 00:00:10,294
the same money walks in the front door,

5
00:00:10,294 --> 00:00:12,254
and somehow it slips out the back.

6
00:00:12,514 --> 00:00:14,415
You don't have an income problem.

7
00:00:14,415 --> 00:00:15,798
You have a leak problem.

8
00:00:16,058 --> 00:00:16,396
Today,

9
00:00:16,396 --> 00:00:19,161
we're going to find every leak in thirty minutes.

10
00:00:20,561 --> 00:00:22,223
Welcome to Broke to Balance.

11
00:00:22,223 --> 00:00:23,886
I'm your guide on this case.

12
00:00:24,146 --> 00:00:26,261
A quick note before we start:

13
00:00:26,261 --> 00:00:27,721
this is educational,

14
00:00:27,721 --> 00:00:29,909
not personal financial advice.

15
00:00:29,909 --> 00:00:31,733
Now, let's open the file.

16
00:00:33,133 --> 00:00:33,709
Step one:

17
00:00:33,709 --> 00:00:34,989
gather the evidence.

18
00:00:35,249 --> 00:00:37,583
Download the last sixty to ninety days

19
00:00:37,583 --> 00:00:39,426
of transactions from your bank

20
00:00:39,426 --> 00:00:41,023
accounts and credit cards.

21
00:00:41,283 --> 00:00:43,893
Most banks let you export a spreadsheet.

22
00:00:43,893 --> 00:00:45,003
If yours doesn't,

23
00:00:45,003 --> 00:00:47,156
copy the lines into one yourself.

24
00:00:47,416 --> 00:00:49,103
Why sixty to ninety days?

25
00:00:49,103 --> 00:00:51,331
Because one month can be unusual.

26
00:00:51,331 --> 00:00:53,896
Three months shows your real patterns.

27
00:00:55,296 --> 00:00:55,893
Step two:

28
00:00:55,893 --> 00:00:58,218
sort every line into three buckets,

29
00:00:58,218 --> 00:01:00,543
the same ones from our first video:

30
00:01:00,543 --> 00:01:02,269
needs, wants, and savings.

31
00:01:02,529 --> 00:01:03,445
In our example,

32
00:01:03,445 --> 00:01:05,885
take-home pay is three thousand dollars.

33
00:01:06,145 --> 00:01:08,248
The audit finds needs at sixteen

34
00:01:08,248 --> 00:01:09,694
hundred forty dollars,

35
00:01:09,694 --> 00:01:11,731
wants at eleven hundred eighty,

36
00:01:11,731 --> 00:01:14,294
and savings at just one hundred eighty.

37
00:01:14,554 --> 00:01:16,721
Compare that to the fifty, thirty,

38
00:01:16,721 --> 00:01:17,677
twenty targets:

39
00:01:17,677 --> 00:01:19,590
fifteen hundred, nine hundred,

40
00:01:19,590 --> 00:01:20,609
and six hundred.

41
00:01:20,869 --> 00:01:21,387
The gap:

42
00:01:21,387 --> 00:01:23,522
needs are one hundred forty over,

43
00:01:23,522 --> 00:01:25,722
wants are two hundred eighty over,

44
00:01:25,722 --> 00:01:28,440
and savings are four hundred twenty short.

45
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:30,706
That's the story the numbers tell.

46
00:01:30,706 --> 00:01:32,359
Now let's find the culprits.

47
00:01:41,359 --> 00:01:41,950
Exhibit A:

48
00:01:41,950 --> 00:01:42,778
subscriptions.

49
00:01:42,778 --> 00:01:44,315
These are the quiet leaks,

50
00:01:44,315 --> 00:01:47,093
because they bill you while you're not looking.

51
00:01:47,353 --> 00:01:49,313
List every recurring charge,

52
00:01:49,313 --> 00:01:52,253
then multiply the monthly price by twelve.

53
00:01:52,253 --> 00:01:53,723
That's the real cost.

54
00:01:53,983 --> 00:01:54,936
In our example,

55
00:01:54,936 --> 00:01:56,715
five subscriptions add up to

56
00:01:56,715 --> 00:01:58,493
sixty-eight dollars a month.

57
00:01:58,493 --> 00:02:01,669
That's about eight hundred fifteen dollars a year.

58
00:02:01,929 --> 00:02:04,436
Now ask three questions about each one.

59
00:02:04,436 --> 00:02:05,979
Did I use it this month?

60
00:02:05,979 --> 00:02:07,779
Would I sign up again today?

61
00:02:07,779 --> 00:02:09,321
Is there a cheaper plan?

62
00:02:09,581 --> 00:02:10,541
Cancel, pause,

63
00:02:10,541 --> 00:02:12,803
or downgrade anything that fails.

64
00:02:14,203 --> 00:02:14,910
Exhibit B:

65
00:02:14,910 --> 00:02:16,042
the small stuff.

66
00:02:16,042 --> 00:02:18,165
Coffee, snacks, delivery fees.

67
00:02:18,425 --> 00:02:20,935
Nobody feels a seven dollar purchase.

68
00:02:20,935 --> 00:02:23,514
But seven dollars, three times a week,

69
00:02:23,514 --> 00:02:26,499
is about one thousand ninety dollars a year.

70
00:02:26,759 --> 00:02:29,174
That's not a reason to give up coffee.

71
00:02:29,174 --> 00:02:31,272
It's a reason to choose the small

72
00:02:31,272 --> 00:02:32,860
purchases you truly love,

73
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:35,085
and cut the ones you barely notice.

74
00:02:36,485 --> 00:02:37,171
Exhibit C:

75
00:02:37,171 --> 00:02:38,406
impulse purchases.

76
00:02:38,406 --> 00:02:41,083
The fix isn't willpower, it's friction.

77
00:02:41,343 --> 00:02:41,900
Rule one:

78
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:43,507
the twenty-four hour rule.

79
00:02:43,507 --> 00:02:44,682
Put it in the cart,

80
00:02:44,682 --> 00:02:46,537
and wait a day before you buy.

81
00:02:46,797 --> 00:02:47,380
Rule two:

82
00:02:47,380 --> 00:02:48,482
for bigger items,

83
00:02:48,482 --> 00:02:50,298
keep a thirty-day wish list.

84
00:02:50,298 --> 00:02:52,567
If you still want it after a month,

85
00:02:52,567 --> 00:02:54,252
it's probably a real want.

86
00:02:54,512 --> 00:02:55,298
Rule three:

87
00:02:55,298 --> 00:02:58,726
remove saved cards from shopping sites and apps,

88
00:02:58,726 --> 00:03:01,155
so buying takes a few extra steps.

89
00:03:01,415 --> 00:03:02,376
And rule four:

90
00:03:02,376 --> 00:03:05,055
unsubscribe from promotional emails and

91
00:03:05,055 --> 00:03:07,252
turn off shopping notifications.

92
00:03:07,252 --> 00:03:09,038
If you can't see the sale,

93
00:03:09,038 --> 00:03:10,343
it can't tempt you.

94
00:03:11,743 --> 00:03:12,493
Exhibit D:

95
00:03:12,493 --> 00:03:12,868
fees.

96
00:03:12,868 --> 00:03:15,494
These are the leaks nobody chooses.

97
00:03:15,754 --> 00:03:18,380
Late fees, overdraft fees, ATM fees,

98
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:20,423
and credit card interest can

99
00:03:20,423 --> 00:03:22,392
quietly drain your account.

100
00:03:22,652 --> 00:03:25,685
Set up autopay for at least the minimum payment,

101
00:03:25,685 --> 00:03:27,644
and turn on low-balance alerts.

102
00:03:27,904 --> 00:03:29,678
Use your bank's own ATMs,

103
00:03:29,678 --> 00:03:32,020
and if you do get hit with a fee,

104
00:03:32,020 --> 00:03:34,503
call and ask for a one-time waiver.

105
00:03:34,503 --> 00:03:36,206
Many banks will say yes.

106
00:03:37,606 --> 00:03:38,337
Exhibit D:

107
00:03:38,337 --> 00:03:40,383
the needs you can negotiate.

108
00:03:40,643 --> 00:03:42,394
Needs aren't always fixed.

109
00:03:42,394 --> 00:03:44,213
Insurance, phone, internet,

110
00:03:44,213 --> 00:03:47,715
and even some utilities can often be shopped around.

111
00:03:47,975 --> 00:03:49,215
Call your providers,

112
00:03:49,215 --> 00:03:52,129
ask what discounts or cheaper plans they offer,

113
00:03:52,129 --> 00:03:53,307
and compare quotes.

114
00:03:53,567 --> 00:03:56,671
If you trim just twenty dollars from three bills,

115
00:03:56,671 --> 00:03:58,508
that's sixty dollars a month,

116
00:03:58,508 --> 00:04:00,978
or seven hundred twenty dollars a year.

117
00:04:02,378 --> 00:04:02,990
Step three:

118
00:04:02,990 --> 00:04:05,048
decide where the recovered money goes

119
00:04:05,048 --> 00:04:06,493
before you spend it again.

120
00:04:06,753 --> 00:04:09,428
Say you cancel two subscriptions for about

121
00:04:09,428 --> 00:04:11,275
thirty-eight dollars a month,

122
00:04:11,275 --> 00:04:13,440
and trim small purchases by sixty.

123
00:04:13,440 --> 00:04:16,178
That's roughly one hundred dollars a month.

124
00:04:16,438 --> 00:04:18,937
Set an automatic transfer on payday:

125
00:04:18,937 --> 00:04:21,296
straight into your emergency fund,

126
00:04:21,296 --> 00:04:22,337
or toward debt.

127
00:04:22,597 --> 00:04:23,260
Over a year,

128
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:25,249
that's about twelve hundred dollars,

129
00:04:25,249 --> 00:04:26,740
from a thirty-minute audit.

130
00:04:34,940 --> 00:04:36,680
Let's see what one audit can do.

131
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:40,280
After trimming sixty dollars from negotiated bills,

132
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:42,179
and ninety-eight dollars from

133
00:04:42,179 --> 00:04:44,406
subscriptions and small purchases,

134
00:04:44,406 --> 00:04:46,501
our example frees up one hundred

135
00:04:46,501 --> 00:04:48,335
fifty-eight dollars a month.

136
00:04:48,595 --> 00:04:51,287
Savings jump from one hundred eighty to three

137
00:04:51,287 --> 00:04:53,501
hundred thirty-eight dollars a month.

138
00:04:53,501 --> 00:04:55,954
That's nearly double, with no new income.

139
00:04:56,214 --> 00:04:59,248
It's still short of the six hundred dollar target,

140
00:04:59,248 --> 00:05:00,219
and that's fine.

141
00:05:00,219 --> 00:05:02,768
The first audit is always the biggest win,

142
00:05:02,768 --> 00:05:05,074
and you can run it again next quarter.

143
00:05:06,474 --> 00:05:08,546
Here's the part most audits miss:

144
00:05:08,546 --> 00:05:09,928
don't cut all the fun.

145
00:05:10,188 --> 00:05:13,203
A budget with zero fun gets abandoned fast.

146
00:05:13,203 --> 00:05:15,447
Instead, give your wants a home.

147
00:05:15,707 --> 00:05:17,311
Set a monthly fun budget,

148
00:05:17,311 --> 00:05:19,750
move it to a separate account or card,

149
00:05:19,750 --> 00:05:22,124
and spend from that and nothing else.

150
00:05:22,384 --> 00:05:24,079
When the fun money runs out,

151
00:05:24,079 --> 00:05:25,834
you're done until next month.

152
00:05:25,834 --> 00:05:26,983
And while it lasts,

153
00:05:26,983 --> 00:05:28,980
you can spend it with zero guilt.

154
00:05:30,380 --> 00:05:31,831
Here's a fun challenge:

155
00:05:31,831 --> 00:05:33,597
a seven day spending freeze.

156
00:05:33,857 --> 00:05:36,121
For one week, spend only on needs:

157
00:05:36,121 --> 00:05:38,984
rent, groceries, utilities, transportation,

158
00:05:38,984 --> 00:05:39,916
and insurance.

159
00:05:40,176 --> 00:05:42,885
Each day, write down what you almost bought.

160
00:05:42,885 --> 00:05:44,485
Those notes are pure gold.

161
00:05:44,745 --> 00:05:45,924
At the end of the week,

162
00:05:45,924 --> 00:05:48,178
you'll know which wants you actually missed,

163
00:05:48,178 --> 00:05:50,484
and which ones you never thought about again.

164
00:05:51,884 --> 00:05:53,953
What should you use to track all this?

165
00:05:53,953 --> 00:05:55,642
There's no single right answer.

166
00:05:55,902 --> 00:05:57,913
A spreadsheet is free, flexible,

167
00:05:57,913 --> 00:06:00,617
and shows you exactly how the numbers work.

168
00:06:00,877 --> 00:06:02,477
Budgeting apps can import

169
00:06:02,477 --> 00:06:04,204
transactions automatically,

170
00:06:04,204 --> 00:06:06,828
but check their fees and privacy policies

171
00:06:06,828 --> 00:06:08,876
before connecting your accounts.

172
00:06:09,136 --> 00:06:11,333
And plain pen and paper works too.

173
00:06:11,333 --> 00:06:13,336
The best tool is the one you'll

174
00:06:13,336 --> 00:06:14,951
actually open every week.

175
00:06:16,351 --> 00:06:18,311
A few audit mistakes to avoid.

176
00:06:18,571 --> 00:06:20,801
Don't turn it into a shame spiral.

177
00:06:20,801 --> 00:06:23,163
You're an investigator, not a judge.

178
00:06:23,163 --> 00:06:24,737
The goal is information.

179
00:06:24,997 --> 00:06:26,650
Don't cut everything at once.

180
00:06:26,650 --> 00:06:28,588
Pick two or three leaks, fix them,

181
00:06:28,588 --> 00:06:29,785
and build from there.

182
00:06:30,045 --> 00:06:31,743
Watch for annual charges,

183
00:06:31,743 --> 00:06:33,780
like insurance or memberships,

184
00:06:33,780 --> 00:06:36,360
which hide between monthly statements.

185
00:06:36,620 --> 00:06:38,738
And repeat the audit every quarter.

186
00:06:38,738 --> 00:06:39,706
Leaks grow back.

187
00:06:47,506 --> 00:06:48,820
Let's close the case.

188
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:51,039
Gather ninety days of evidence.

189
00:06:51,299 --> 00:06:53,795
Sort it into needs, wants, and savings.

190
00:06:54,055 --> 00:06:56,278
Hunt the subscriptions, the small stuff,

191
00:06:56,278 --> 00:06:57,445
and the impulse buys.

192
00:06:57,705 --> 00:06:59,608
Then automate what you recover.

193
00:06:59,868 --> 00:07:01,786
Next time, we go head to head:

194
00:07:01,786 --> 00:07:04,087
debt snowball versus debt avalanche.

195
00:07:04,347 --> 00:07:05,781
If this helped, subscribe,

196
00:07:05,781 --> 00:07:07,326
and tell me in the comments:

197
00:07:07,326 --> 00:07:08,816
what was your biggest leak?

198
00:07:09,076 --> 00:07:11,493
And if you learned something from this video,

199
00:07:11,493 --> 00:07:13,480
don't forget to watch our next video.

200
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:16,356
This video is for education only and

201
00:07:16,356 --> 00:07:18,027
isn't financial advice.

202
00:07:18,027 --> 00:07:18,899
Case closed.

